"(PDF) Identification of sulfate starvation-regulated genes in Escherichia coli: A gene cluster involved in the utilization of taurine as a sulfur source (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/14395828_Identification_of_sulfate_starvation-regulated_genes_in_Escherichia_coli_A_gene_cluster_involved_in_the_utilization_of_taurine_as_a_sulfur_source)\nciteturn35view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 9 months ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/14395828_Identification_of_sulfate_starvation-regulated_genes_in_Escherichia_coli_A_gene_cluster_involved_in_the_utilization_of_taurine_as_a_sulfur_source\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 2682\nL0: Article PDF Available\nL1: # Identification of sulfate starvation-regulated genes in Escherichia coli: A gene cluster involved in the utilization of taurine as a sulfur source\nL2: \nL3: 【0† Journal of Bacteriology】\nL4: \nL5:   * October 1996\nL6:   * 178(18):5438-46\nL7: \nL8: DOI:【1†10.1128/jb.178.18.5438-5446.1996†doi.org】\nL9: \nL10:   * Source\nL11:   * 【2†PubMed】\nL12: \nL13: Authors:\nL14: \nL15: 【3† 】\nL16: \nL17: 【3†Jan R van der Ploeg】\nL18: \nL19:   * 【4†University of Zurich】\nL20: \nL21: 【5† 】\nL22: \nL23: 【5†MA Weiss】\nL24: \nL25: 【5†MA Weiss】\nL26: \nL27:   * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\nL28: \nL29: 【6† 】\nL30: \nL31: 【6†Elisabeth Saller】\nL32:   * 【7†Medical Research Council (UKRI)】\nL33: \nL34: 【8† 】\nL35: \nL36: 【8†H Nashimoto】\nL37: \nL38: 【8†H Nashimoto】\nL39: \nL40:   * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\nL41: \nL42: Show all 7 authors Hide\nL43: \nL44: 【143†Image†www.researchgate.net】\nL45: \nL46: 【9†Download full-text PDF】【10†Read full-text】\nL47: \nL48: 【11†Download full-text PDF】\nL49: \nL50: 【10†Read full-text】\nL51: \nL52: 【12†Download citation】\nL53: \nL54: Copy link Link copied\nL55: \nL56: * * *\nL57: \nL58: 【10† Read full-text】【12† Download citation】\nL59: \nL60: Copy link Link copied\nL61: \nL62: 【13†Citations (188)】【14†References (65)】【15†Figures (7)】\nL63: ## Abstract and Figures\nL64: Genes whose expression is regulated by sulfate starvation in Escherichia coli were identified by generating random translational lacZ fusions in the chromosome with the lambda placMu9 system. Nine lacZ fusion strains which expressed beta-galactosidase after growth under sulfate starvation conditions but not after growth in the presence of sulfate were found.\nL65: These included two strains with insertions in the dmsA and rhsD genes, respectively, and seven strains in which the insertions were located within a 1.8-kb region downstream of hemB at 8.5 minutes on the E. coli chromosome. Analysis of the nucleotide sequence of this region indicated the presence of four open reading frames designated tauABCD.\nL66: Disruption of these genes resulted in the loss of the ability to utilize taurine (2-aminoethanesulfonate) as a source of sulfur but did not affect the utilization of a range of other aliphatic sulfonates as sulfur sources.\nL67: The TauA protein contained a putative signal peptide for transport into the periplasm; the TauB and TauC proteins showed sequence similarity to ATP-binding proteins and membrane proteins, respectively, of ABC-type transport systems; and the TauD protein was related in sequence to a dichlorophenoxyacetic acid dioxygenase.\nL68: We therefore suggest that the proteins encoded by tauABC constitute an uptake system for taurine and that the product of tauD is involved in the oxygenolytic release of sulfite from taurine. The transcription initiation site was detected 26 to 27 bp upstream of the translational start site of tauA. Expression of the tauD gene was dependent on CysB, the transcriptional activator of the cysteine regulon.\nL69: 【16† 】\nL70: 【16†Nucleotide sequence of the NsiI-HpaI fragment encompassing tauABCD and deduced amino acid sequences of translated ORFs. Relevant restriction sites are shown above the sequence. Putative ribosome binding sites are double underlined. The positions of placMu9 insertions are indicated (inverted triangles), and the position of box C (7) is shown. The N-terminal and internal peptide sequences of proteins Ssi1 and Ssi3 (38) are underlined and in boldface print.\nL71: The transcriptional start sites are indicated by arrows. … 】\nL72: 【17† 】\nL73: \nL74: 【17†PILEUP amino acid sequence alignment of TauB with the sequences of the ATP-binding proteins OppD (accession no. P04285) and HisP (accession no. P02915) from S. typhimurium and MalK (accession no. P02914) from E. coli. Residues identical in three or all of the sequences are shown in black. The Walker motifs (61) are underlined and indicated as WA and WB. … 】\nL75: \nL76: 【18† 】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Identification of sulfate starvation-regulated genes in Escherichia coli: A gene cluster involved in the utilization of taurine as a sulfur source (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/14395828_Identification_of_sulfate_starvation-regulated_genes_in_Escherichia_coli_A_gene_cluster_involved_in_the_utilization_of_taurine_as_a_sulfur_source)\nciteturn35view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 9 months ago; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn34search0\",\"pattern\":\"containing 64\"}); Total lines: 2682\nL315: sulfonate (24, 38). These proteins have been named sulfate\nL316: \nL317: starvation-induced (SSI) proteins. By comparison of the N-\nL318: \nL319: terminal and internal peptide sequences of the SSI proteins\nL320: \nL321: with nucleotide and protein databases, three of the proteins\nL322: \nL323: were identiﬁed as the sulfate-binding protein, the general cys-\nL324: \nL325: tine-binding protein, and O-acetyl serine (thiol)lyase A (38). In\nL326: \nL327: enterobacteria synthesis of these proteins is known to be reg-\nL328: \nL329: ulated as part of the cys regulon (29).\nL330: Here we describe, as an alternative to the identiﬁcation of\nL331: \nL332: SSI proteins by two-dimensional gel electrophoresis, the isola-\nL333: \nL334: tion, characterization, and mapping of several sulfate-regu-\nL335: \nL336: lated lacZ fusions which have been generated with l p lac Mu9.\nL337: \nL338: The majority of these lacZ fusions are located in the tauABCD\nL339: \nL340: genes, which are required for the utilization of taurine as a\nL341: \nL342: sulfur source and whose products are thought to constitute an\nL343: \nL344: ABC transport system for taurine plus an enzyme involved in\nL345: the desulfonation of this compound.\nL346: \nL347: MATERIALS AND METHODS\nL348: \nL349: Chemicals. All chemicals used as sulfur sources were of the highest quality\nL350: \nL351: available and were obtained from Fluka (Buchs, Switzerland), except for isethio-\nL352: \nL353: nate, lanthionine, and methyl sulfate, which were purchased from Aldrich\nL354: \nL355: (Buchs, Switzerland). Oligonucleotides were obtained from Microsynth (Bal-\nL356: \nL357: gach, Switzerland) or Intron (Kaltbrunn, Switzerland).\nL358: \nL359: Bacterial strains and growth conditions. Strains of E. coli, plasmids, and\nL360: phages used in this study are listed in Table 1. All E. coli strains were grown at\nL361: \nL362: 37 8 C either in Luria-Bertani (LB) medium (43) or in a modiﬁed M63 medium\nL363: \nL364: (33) containing 64 mM potassium phosphate (pH 7.2), 33 mM NH\nL365: \nL366: 4\nL367: \nL368: Cl, 1%\nL369: \nL370: (vol/vol) sulfur-free trace elements (54), 1 mM MgCl\nL371: \nL372: 2\nL373: \nL374: , 0.3 mM thiamine, and\nL375: \nL376: 0.2% (wt/vol) glucose. Sulfur sources were added as described below to a ﬁnal\nL377: \nL378: concentration of 250 m M. When necessary, kanamycin chloride (25 m g/ml) was\nL379: added to the growth medium. Since kanamycin is commercially available only as\nL380: \nL381: a sulfate salt, the counterion was replaced by chloride by passage over a Dowex\nL382: \nL383: 1 3 8 column (Cl\nL384: \nL385: 2\nL386: \nL387: form), elution with water, and lyophilization. Kanamycin\nL388: \nL389: chloride was tested for residual sulfate by ion chromatography (5) and for\nL390: \nL391: antibiotic activity before further use. Other antibiotics were added at the follow-\nL392: \nL393: ing concentrations: ampicillin, 100 m g/ml, and tetracycline, 12.5 m g/ml. Trypto-\nL394: phan was added at 100 m g/ml. Sulfur-limited solid media were prepared by\nL395: \nL396: addition of 0.6% SeaPlaque agarose (FMC BioProducts); 5-bromo-4-chloro-3-\nL397: \nL398: indolyl galactoside (X-Gal; 80 m g/ml) was added when necessary.\nL399: \nL400: TABLE 1. E. coli strains, phages, and plasmids used in this study\nL401: \nL402: Strain, phage, or\nL403: \nL404: plasmid Relevant features Reference or source\nL405: \nL406: Strains\nL407: \nL408: MC4100 F\nL409: \nL410: 2\nL411: \nL412: araD139 D(argF-lac)U169 rpsL150 relA1 deoC1 ptsF25 rbsR ﬂbB5301 11\nL413: \nL414: MBM7014 F\nL415: \nL416: 2\nL417: araC(Am) araD139 D(argF-lac)U169 trp(Am) mal(Am) rpsL150 relA1 thi supF G. M. Weinstock\nL418: \nL419: MW-11 MC4100 F(tauC::lacZ)(l p lac Mu9) This study\nL420: \nL421: MW-15 MC4100 F(tauC::lacZ)(l p lac Mu9) This study\nL422: \nL423: MW-43 MC4100 F(tauC::lacZ)(l p lac Mu9) This study\nL424: \nL425: MW-61 MC4100 F(tauB::lacZ)(l p lac Mu9) This study\nL426: \nL427: MW-74 MC4100 F(tauD::lacZ)(l p lac Mu9) This study\nL428: \nL429: MW-82 MC4100 F(tauD::lacZ)(l p lac Mu9) This study\nL430: \nL431: MW-103 MC4100 F(rhsD::lacZ)(l p lac Mu9) This study\nL432: MW-108 MC4100 F(tauD::lacZ)(l p lac Mu9) This study\nL433: \nL434: MW-109 MC4100 F(dmsA::lacZ)(l p lac Mu9) This study\nL435: \nL436: DH5 a supE44 D lacU169 (f 80 lacZ D M15)hsdR17 recA1 endA1 gyrA96 thi-1 relA1 Laboratory collection\nL437: \nL438: MDA4762 cysB trpB::Tn 10 46\nL439: \nL440: MW-108 cysB MW-108 cysB trpB::Tn 10 This study\nL441: \nL442: Phages\nL443: \nL444: l p lac Mu9 imm l9 lacZ lacY\nL445: \nL446: 1\nL447: \nL448: lacA 99 ara 9 Xho::k